{
  "id": "pc-calculus-02",
  "version": 2,
  "title": "Sequences and series",
  "xp": 20,
  "tags": [
    "precalculus",
    "sequences",
    "series"
  ],
  "learn": [
    {
      "id": "intro",
      "kind": "text",
      "title": "Lists with a rule",
      "body": "A sequence is an ordered list of numbers. An arithmetic sequence adds the same amount $d$ each time: $a_n=a_1+(n-1)d$. A geometric sequence multiplies by the same ratio $r$: $a_n=a_1r^{n-1}$."
    },
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "kind": "text",
      "title": "Adding terms: series",
      "body": "A series is a sum of terms. For arithmetic, $S_n=\\frac{n(a_1+a_n)}{2}$. For geometric with $r\\ne1$, $S_n=\\frac{a_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$. Use $\\sum$ notation to write them."
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "kind": "example",
      "title": "An arithmetic series",
      "body": "Sequence $3,7,11,\\ldots$ with 10 terms.",
      "steps": [
        "$d=4$, so $a_{10}=3+9\\cdot4=39$",
        "$S_{10}=\\frac{10(3+39)}{2}=210$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p3",
      "kind": "example",
      "title": "A geometric series",
      "body": "Sequence $2,6,18,\\ldots$ with 5 terms.",
      "steps": [
        "$r=3$",
        "$S_5=\\frac{2(1-3^5)}{1-3}=\\frac{2(-242)}{-2}=242$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p4",
      "kind": "text",
      "title": "Infinite sums",
      "body": "If $|r|<1$ the terms shrink and the infinite sum is $\\frac{a_1}{1-r}$. For $1+\\frac12+\\frac14+\\ldots$ it is $\\frac{1}{1-1/2}=2$. If $|r|\\ge1$ the sum does not settle."
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
